553
00:39:13,741 --> 00:39:14,142
Scott: Okay.

554
00:39:14,182 --> 00:39:15,184
Scott: Well, you can go to the store and buy some.

555
00:39:15,204 --> 00:39:21,340
Rym: That's a cute high school thing, but smells are intensely tied to human memory.

556
00:39:22,741 --> 00:39:23,143
Rym: Very.

557
00:39:23,283 --> 00:39:27,855
Rym: To a degree that we are not going to explain because we're not experts in this, go read Wikipedia, but it's real.

558
00:39:28,096 --> 00:39:29,700
Rym: Like this is a profound thing.

559
00:39:30,200 --> 00:39:35,452
Scott: So for some reason, the sense of smell is more tied to memory than other senses.

560
00:39:35,532 --> 00:39:35,672
Scott: Right.

561
00:39:35,692 --> 00:39:39,120
Scott: If I see something I've seen before, you know, I might remember it.

562
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:40,944
Scott: If I hear something I've heard before, I remember it.

563
00:39:40,984 --> 00:39:47,680
Scott: But if I smell something I've smelled before, my brain will like go back and find like the moment that I smelled that same smell.

564
00:39:48,180 --> 00:39:52,350
Rym: So broadly, how that works is kind of fascinating.

565
00:39:52,550 --> 00:39:56,740
Rym: I'll get the shortest summary because this is something I've read a lot about over the last couple of years.

566
00:39:57,280 --> 00:40:04,215
Rym: Basically, your brain is sort of ticking periodically and like linking a bunch of neurons to form memories.

567
00:40:04,275 --> 00:40:06,660
Rym: And it has this like clock rate almost to forming memories.

568
00:40:07,081 --> 00:40:12,973
Rym: And each memory is linked to sets of sensations that are happening at the same time.

569
00:40:13,594 --> 00:40:16,660
Rym: But sense are like they're all arbitrary.

570
00:40:16,860 --> 00:40:18,223
Rym: They're all treated kind of the same.

571
00:40:18,663 --> 00:40:27,600
Rym: But because sense are rarer to be strong and due to a bunch of other factors, you'll tend to remember things where you smell.

572
00:40:28,120 --> 00:40:33,780
Rym: Memories where you smelled the same thing at that time will be connected to each other, even if the only thing connecting them is the smell.

573
00:40:34,241 --> 00:40:40,554
Rym: And that's part of it because our brain just takes this cadence of here's 10 bits, here's 10 bits, here's 10 bits.

574
00:40:40,834 --> 00:40:43,720
Rym: And then it just makes like this lateral database across those bits.

575
00:40:44,280 --> 00:41:00,280
Scott: The point is, if you can get a bottle of liquid that smells very close to or exactly the way something smelled when you had a positive time memory in your life and you want to evoke that memory again, that is a useful thing to have around.

576
00:41:01,362 --> 00:41:06,573
Rym: I just imagine the smell of magic cards and vivid memories of specific times.

577
00:41:06,653 --> 00:41:07,956
Rym: I played magic in middle school.

578
00:41:07,976 --> 00:41:09,719
Rym: It came flooding back into my brain just now.

579
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:12,442
Rym: Just by imagining the smell of a magic card.

580
00:41:13,964 --> 00:41:29,340
Rym: But I've seen, I was reading a cool Reddit thread of people who are trying to hack their brains to improve their memory or to otherwise get better at certain video games by using sense to encode and help them remember specific kinds of things.

581
00:41:30,147 --> 00:41:31,460
Scott: You probably could do that to some extent.

582
00:41:31,460 --> 00:41:32,063
Rym: You probably could do that.

583
00:41:32,364 --> 00:41:34,112
Rym: Think about the old string around the finger.

584
00:41:34,153 --> 00:41:35,740
Rym: that was the thing before smartphones existed.

585
00:41:37,121 --> 00:41:44,840
Scott: So one thing I do worry about with fragrances is that the fragrance industry has had a history of animal testings.

586
00:41:45,661 --> 00:41:47,760
Rym: A lot of it's gone away from that recently.

587
00:41:47,940 --> 00:41:58,740
